Let $L$ be the number of large jelly beans Grandpa can eat, $M$ be the number of medium jelly beans Grandpa can eat, and $S$ be the number of small jelly beans Grandpa can eat.
We are given that it takes 75 large jelly beans to fill Grandpa up, so $L = 75$.
Also, he can eat twice as many medium-sized beans as large beans, so $M = 2L = 2(75) = 150$.
Eating 3 small beans is the same as eating 1 medium-sized bean, so $3S = 1M$.
Therefore, $3S = 1(150)$, which gives us $3S = 150$.
Dividing both sides by 3, we get $S = \frac{150}{3} = 50$.
So Grandpa can eat 50 small jelly beans.

